From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: 2026-09-03
Subject: [PATCH 017/NNN] use the portable spellings for ifreq and in6_addr members

Three spellings here are glibc-specific where a portable one exists and
means the same thing.

ifr_ifrn.ifrn_name reaches into the union directly. Linux defines ifr_name
as a macro for exactly that member and the BSD struct spells it that way to
begin with, so ifr_name is correct on both.

ifr_netmask and ifr_addr name different members of the same union at the
same offset on Linux, and SIOCSIFNETMASK reads the mask out of ifr_addr on
BSD, so ifr_addr works on both.

in6_u.u6_addr8 is the glibc member name. Both glibc and Darwin define
s6_addr as a macro for their own spelling of it.

Nothing here changes behaviour on Linux.

--- a//tmp/gw.cc.orig5	2026-09-03 15:45:40
+++ b/srsue/src/stack/upper/gw.cc	2026-09-03 15:48:51
@@ -431,8 +431,8 @@
   memset(&ifr, 0, sizeof(ifr));
   ifr.ifr_flags = IFF_TUN | IFF_NO_PI;
   strncpy(
-      ifr.ifr_ifrn.ifrn_name, args.tun_dev_name.c_str(), std::min(args.tun_dev_name.length(), (size_t)(IFNAMSIZ - 1)));
-  ifr.ifr_ifrn.ifrn_name[IFNAMSIZ - 1] = 0;
+      ifr.ifr_name, args.tun_dev_name.c_str(), std::min(args.tun_dev_name.length(), (size_t)(IFNAMSIZ - 1)));
+  ifr.ifr_name[IFNAMSIZ - 1] = 0;
   if (0 > ioctl(tun_fd, TUNSETIFF, &ifr)) {
     err_str = strerror(errno);
     logger.error("Failed to set TUN device name: %s", err_str);
